all.finding:
The firmware keys this variant exactly the way our code does — by the device TYPE string — and the rationale our comment gives is the one thing the firmware contradicts.

UNIVERSAL_LIGHT_RECEIVER.tcl:21-31 verbatim:

  if {[info exists dev_ps(DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE)] == 1} {
    # HmIP-RGB(W)
    set devMode $dev_ps(DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE); # 0 = RGBW, 1 = RGB, 2 = Tuneable White, 3 = PWM
  } else {
    # DALI
    set devMode 4
  }

  if {[string first "HmIP-LSC" $dev_descr(TYPE)] != -1} {
    set devMode 5
  }

Absence of DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE selects DALI (mode 4), not LSC. LSC is mode 5 and is reached only by the TYPE-string test, which runs AFTER and overrides the descriptor branch. The same two-step appears in hmipUniversalLightReceiverConfigDialogs.tcl:25-27 (this time overriding UNIVERSAL_LIGHT_MAX_CAPABILITIES) and in linkHmIP_UNIVERSAL_LIGHT_RECEIVER.tcl, where PARENT_TYPE == "HmIP-LSC" selects the dedicated easymode directory UNIVERSAL_LIGHT_RECEIVER_LSC while HmIP-RGBW dispatches on DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE and HmIP-DRG-DALI on UNIVERSAL_LIGHT_MAX_CAPABILITIES. hmipChannelConfigDialogs.tcl:102-104 closes it: the CCU renders the DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE selector only when devType == "HmIP-RGBW", i.e. it treats the parameter as HmIP-RGBW-specific.

Tier 2 agrees and shows why the descriptor-absence rationale could not have worked: across the entire pydevccu paramset corpus, HmIP-RGBW is the only device type carrying DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE. HmIP-LSC, HmIP-DRDI3 and HmIP-DRG-DALI all lack it — and our RGBWLight profile serves LSC and DRDI3 too (rgbw.go:62). Had the code followed the comment, HmIP-DRDI3 would have been forced into combined HS+CT behaviour as well.

The bundle's stated damage is therefore refuted: classifying an RGBW-class model without DEVICE_OPERATION_MODE as non-LSC is what the firmware does, not a defect.

What is genuinely narrower than the firmware: our acceptance set. Firmware accepts any TYPE containing "HmIP-LSC" (case-sensitive); we accept only the exact string (case-insensitive). Today no divergence is observable — "HmIP-LSC" is the sole LSC-bearing type in the deviceDescription catalogue (www/webui/js/lang/en/translate.lang.deviceDescription.js:230) and in every data extract I searched. The boundary runs at a future variant type: a hypothetical "HmIP-LSC-2" or an ELV-relabelled SKU whose TYPE embeds the token would take mode 5 in the CCU and RGBW mode in us. A recommendation follows for free from the firmware: strings.Contains, not EqualFold.

reachesDevice is true, not presentational: colorTempCombined gates SetKelvin (rgbw.go:380) and SetColor (rgbw.go:427) via HasColorTemperature/HasColor, so a wrong value rejects a command before it reaches the wire.
